## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1),
submitted as a single batch job. Inputs are read-only; nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. **Resolve inputs.** At run time, resolve both inputs with
   `python3 _system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-2a304f06 --assay rnaseq_bulk --type counts_gene`
   and `--type design`; the script takes the resolved paths as arguments. Abort if either fails.
2. **Load and align.** Read the count matrix (tab-separated; columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`,
   then one column per sample). Index by `gene_id`; keep `gene_name` aside for reporting only.
   Read the design CSV. Require the set of sample columns to equal the design's `sample_id`
   set exactly; abort with the mismatch otherwise. Order sample columns as in the design.
3. **Prepare counts (in memory).** The matrix is salmon length-scaled counts, which are
   non-integer; round to the nearest integer (`numpy.rint`, cast to int) as DESeq2 expects.
   Drop genes with a total count of 0 across all samples. Record the number of genes kept.
4. **Variance-stabilising transform.** Build a `pydeseq2.dds.DeseqDataSet` with the rounded
   counts, the design as metadata and `design="~condition"`; call `dds.vst(use_design=False)`
   (blind VST, DESeq2's `vst(blind=TRUE)` equivalent, so the transform does not see the
   condition labels it will be coloured by). Use the resulting VST matrix (samples x genes).
5. **Gene selection.** Rank genes by variance of the VST values across samples and keep the
   top `n = min(500, number of genes kept)` — DESeq2 `plotPCA`'s default of 500. (The matrix
   currently holds 200 genes, so all kept genes will be used.)
6. **PCA.** Centre each gene (no scaling to unit variance, as in `plotPCA`) and run
   `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` — up to 6 components
   for 6 samples. Record per-component explained-variance ratio.
7. **Plot.** Scatter PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by
   `condition` (two-colour categorical palette, legend titled "condition"), each point
   labelled with its `sample_id`; axis labels give the percentage of variance explained
   (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Title: "Sample PCA — VST, top <n> variable genes". Save as PNG
   (300 dpi) and PDF.
8. **Write tables.** Sample scores on every component, joined to the design columns; and the
   explained-variance ratio per component, with the cumulative sum.

